course: dictionary
lesson: 6962
title: Words about comfort
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are about hard things. You may meet one when someone you love is very poorly. Palliative is one of these words, so read the words around it: «When someone is dying, doctors and nurses, including hospice and palliative care teams, keep them comfortable and free of pain, and support the family.» The clues are comfortable and free of pain. Palliative care is about comfort, not about curing the illness.
explain: Palliative can also describe something that makes a problem feel less bad for now without fixing its cause. Test it by swapping in your meaning. A bucket under a leaking roof is only a palliative: it helps for now, but the leak is still there. A palliative is the noun form, something that eases pain or a problem for a while.
explain: If a hard word makes you feel unsure, it is fine to ask a grown-up what it means. Understanding a sad word can help you understand how people care for each other.
fact: Palliative care is care that keeps a very ill person comfortable and free from pain when their illness cannot be cured.
fact: Palliative means making a problem feel less bad for now without fixing its cause.
fact: A palliative is something that eases pain or a problem for a while.
fact: A «palliative care nurse» is a nurse who helps very ill people stay comfortable.
fact: Palliative is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in health talk or serious writing.
exercise: «When someone is dying, doctors and nurses keep them comfortable and free of pain.» What is the main job of palliative care here? | *Keeping someone comfortable and free from pain | Building a new hospital | Sending the family home | Curing the illness completely
exercise: A bucket under a leaking roof is only a palliative. What does that tell you? | It stops the leak for ever | It makes the roof taller | *It helps for now, but the leak is still there | It is a brand-new roof
exercise: Which word means something that eases a problem for a while? | *a palliative | a cure | a sad story | a hospital bed
test: «The nurse explained that the medicine was palliative, so it would ease the pain but could not cure the illness.» What does palliative mean here? | *Easing pain without curing the illness | Curing the illness for ever | Making the pain worse | A kind of sweet
test: Which sentence uses palliative correctly? | Palliative is a type of bird that sings at night. | *Palliative care keeps a very ill person comfortable. | We went palliative swimming at the lake. | The palliative castle stood on the hill.
exam: What does a palliative do? | Hides from the problem and runs away | Makes things louder and brighter | Fixes the cause for ever | *Eases pain or a problem for a while
